One of Apple's accessibility features on iPhone can listen for important sounds around you, such as a doorbell, siren or alarm, and it sends you a notification when it hears one. The feature has been extended to Apple's new Apple Watch Series 12 and Apple Watch Ultra 4 models, so the alert reaches your wrist even when your iPhone isn't with you.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/watch-sound-recognition.jpg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076893" /><br/>
It's especially useful for people who are deaf or hard of hearing, but it has everyday uses too. If you wear noise-cancelling headphones while working and don't want to miss the doorbell – if you're expecting a delivery, for example – your Apple Watch can alert you.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>Sounds Your Apple Watch Can Detect</h2><br/>
Sound Recognition can detect the following 15 sounds. Any you switch on in the iPhone's Sounds list are also detected by your Apple Watch when "Detect using Apple Watch" is enabled:<br/>
<ul><br/>
<li>Fire Alarm</li><br/>
<li>Siren</li><br/>
<li>Smoke Alarm</li><br/>
<li>Cat</li><br/>
<li>Dog</li><br/>
<li>Appliances</li><br/>
<li>Car Horn</li><br/>
<li>Doorbell</li><br/>
<li>Door Knock</li><br/>
<li>Glass Breaking</li><br/>
<li>Kettle</li><br/>
<li>Water Running</li><br/>
<li>Baby Crying</li><br/>
<li>Coughing</li><br/>
<li>Shouting</li><br/>
</ul>If a sound isn't recognized automatically, you can also teach your iPhone a <strong>Custom Alarm</strong> or <strong>Custom Appliance or Doorbell</strong> from the same Sounds list.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>What You'll Need</h2><br/>
You'll need an Apple Watch Series 12 or Ultra 4 paired with an iPhone 11 or later, or an iPhone SE (2nd generation or later), running iOS 27. If you already use Sound Recognition on your iPhone, it turns on automatically for your Apple Watch, so you may not need to do anything. Either way, here's how to set it up:<br/>
<ol><br/>
<li>Open the <strong>Settings</strong> app on your iPhone and tap <strong>Accessibility</strong>.</li><br/>
<li>In the "Hearing" section, tap <strong>Sound & Name Recognition</strong>.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/sound-recognition-apple-watch2.jpeg" alt="settings" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076890" /><br/>
<li>Tap <strong>Sound Recognition</strong> and turn on the <strong>Sound Recognition</strong> toggle, then turn on <strong>Detect using Apple Watch</strong>.<br/>
<li>Tap <strong>Sounds</strong> and turn on the sounds you want to hear about, such as <strong>Doorbell</strong>. You can also choose a specific alert tone for each sound.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/sound-recognition-apple-watch1.jpeg" alt="settings" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076896" /><br/>
